# Test: an external plugin's command is judged by the YANG it brings, before the plugin is asked
#
# Question: when an external plugin brings a config module, a -cmd module and
# an -api module at Stage 1, and declares the commands at Stage 1, does the
# daemon refuse a value its argument's type refuses (a name longer than 16
# characters) and a missing argument without asking the plugin, while a good
# value reaches the plugin with the value the operator typed?
# Why not a unit test: the answer is the whole path of a running daemon: the
# modules joined at start, the argument definitions rebuilt from the joined
# set, the command typed by an operator over `ze cli -c`, the dispatcher's
# route to a forked process, and the plugin's own answer. Unit tests prove the
# join and the definitions alone (internal/component/config/yang
# join_command_test.go).
#
# The plugin is a REAL external process built on the public Go SDK
# (internal/test/fixture/plugin_fixture_external_yang.go, variant `commands`).
# It prints each command it receives with its arguments, so a value the daemon
# refused is a line that never appears.
#
# VALIDATES: spec-plugin-external-yang-at-startup, the command step: the
#   module list on the wire, the -cmd module joined, and the argument check of
#   route R9 (Dispatcher.routeToProcess) reading the external command's leaf.
# PREVENTS: an external plugin's command reaching the plugin with a value its
#   own YANG refuses, or with no value at all.
#
# Method: the daemon runs one SSH server on 127.0.0.1 $PORT, which the CLI
# dials, as test/plugin/command-arg-enum-refusal.ci does.

option=timeout:value=60s
option=env:var=ZE_CONFIG_DIR:value=.
option=env:var=XDG_DATA_HOME:value=.
option=env:var=ze.ssh.password:value=testpass

stdin=init-answers:terminator=EOF_INIT
admin
testpass
127.0.0.1
$PORT
EOF_INIT

tmpfs=commands.conf:terminator=EOF_CONF
system {
	authentication {
		# bcrypt cost-4 hash of "testpass".
		user admin {
			password "$2a$04$UlwuiuH82Unfsq.XEMPGJeDkXwbm3KW.nvVaVXOd/JeFK8VjMjrQO"
		}
	}
}
environment {
	ssh {
		enabled true
		server main {
			ip 127.0.0.1;
			port $PORT;
		}
	}
}
plugin {
	external acme-monitor {
		run "le test fixture plugin/external-yang commands"
		encoder json
	}
}
acme-monitor {
	target probe-a
	interval 5
}
EOF_CONF

#
# Where each step is observed. A `ze cli -c` is a quick-exit command, so its
# `exit=` is compared and its own output span is exact. The plugin answers
# with the command and the arguments it received (externalYANGCommand), so a
# good step proves the plugin was asked, with the value, in that step's own
# stdout. A refused step proves the refusal came from the argument check by
# its exit and its text, and the rejects under the daemon prove the plugin
# never printed that command: the daemon's span holds every line the plugin's
# stderr relayed, to the end of the run. The refusals run between the two good
# steps, so the last good step's round trip follows them.

cmd=foreground:seq=1:exec=ze init:stdin=init-answers:exit=0
cmd=background:seq=2:exec=ze start commands.conf:name=daemon:ready=Ze running.
expect=stderr:contains=FIXTURE: external-yang command show acme-monitor status args=[]
reject=stderr:contains=args=[this-name-is-far-too-long]
reject=stderr:contains=FIXTURE: external-yang command show acme-monitor target args=[]
reject=stderr:pattern=ZE-OBSERVER-FAIL

# The command with no argument reaches the plugin.
cmd=foreground:seq=3:exec=ze cli -c "show acme-monitor status":exit=0
expect=stdout:contains=show acme-monitor status
expect=stdout:contains=[]

# A value longer than the leaf's length bound (25 characters, the leaf takes
# 1..16): refused by the daemon's argument check, with the whole command as
# the fix, and never sent to the plugin.
cmd=foreground:seq=4:exec=ze cli -c "show acme-monitor target this-name-is-far-too-long":exit=1
expect=stderr:contains=is outside the accepted length (25 characters)
expect=stderr:contains=name accepts 1..16 characters
expect=stderr:contains=fix: show acme-monitor target

# The argument is mandatory: a line without it is refused by the same check,
# with the missing-argument refusal and not the length one. Today that refusal
# is its problem line alone, with no why and no fix (journal
# `error-names-no-fix-command.md`, 2026-11-17), so no fix line is asserted
# here; the reject under the daemon proves the plugin was not asked.
cmd=foreground:seq=5:exec=ze cli -c "show acme-monitor target":exit=1
expect=stderr:contains=error: required argument missing: name
reject=stderr:contains=is outside the accepted length

# A good value reaches the plugin as typed: the plugin's answer names it.
cmd=foreground:seq=6:exec=ze cli -c "show acme-monitor target probe-b":exit=0
expect=stdout:contains=show acme-monitor target
expect=stdout:contains=[probe-b]
